{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgH3b9Q6qwUY1rufvFkav","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014YAQt5ngVYBHPcSUSQajET","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-969\\docs\\reviews\\wi-969\\007-ADJUDICATE-9d73112.md","content":"# WI-969 checkpoint sitting at 9d73112\n\n## amendment\n\nThis sitting re-sits the set of sitting 006-ADJUDICATE-e213f8d. That sitting blessed SR-148, LLR-161, LLR-312 and TC-254, and returned LLR-153, TC-147 and TC-156. Commit 9d73112ca answers the returns. I compared every judged row at e213f8d7f and at this tip, field by field. Only the three returned rows changed, and only in the cells returned: LLR-153's Detail, TC-147's Expected, and TC-156's Expected and Method. The other four amended rows are byte-identical to the text blessed at 006. All seven rows changed MEANING against their anchor (b70ecbe9e), and I would bless all seven. The rung is released, so the later act turn re-attests each.\n\n- [MEANING+BLESS] SR-148 AcceptanceCriteria -> before: processing an unresolved handback resolves the record or mints a provenance-linked successor exactly once -> after: the same, or it names an already-open work item that supersedes the closed work item as its successor -> not the same: a third discharge enters the acceptance. Unchanged since sitting 006 blessed it: an observable condition naming no file, decomposed by LLR-335 and LLR-345.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] LLR-161 Detail -> before: the report-first close, one disposition per partial or cancelled close, and a continuing successor drafted with lineage -> after: the same, plus a disposition owing a successor drafts one or adopts an already-filed superseding row -> not the same: adoption is newly admissible. Unchanged since sitting 006 blessed it: one sentence, with validation and recording left to LLR-335.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] LLR-153 Detail -> before: the mint invariant, the three triggers, drafts-not-mints and the dial arms -> after: the same, plus:\n  - an adopts-only block allocates no id;\n  - a standing row found by exact title whose lineage (its `supersedes`) differs from the draft's refuses the mint before any write, while one on the draft's own lineage stands;\n  - every replay re-points to the full successor set.\n\n  -> not the same: three new mint obligations. Ready to bless. Sitting 006's finding is answered: the mismatch outcome is now stated as a refusal before any write, which is WI-902's Trust ruling, with the same-lineage case standing. The row now says where it refuses, and no new symbol name is added.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] TC-147 Expected and Method -> before: the intake suite's trigger, draft, open-item, census, context and dial arms, via LLR-153 -> after: the same, plus: an adopts-only block allocates no id; a title match on another lineage refuses before any write while one on the draft's own lineage is kept; a replay re-points a late dependent to the full successor set, drafts and adoptions together, committing nothing when nothing is left -> not the same: new arms enter the verified set. Ready to bless. The mis-citation is answered: Expected now places full-successor replay under LLR-153's replay clause and LLR-335's single-lineage operation, where those obligations actually live, not under SR-148. Expected's short phrase \"title identity and lineage resolve together\" is pinned by the Method's explicit refusal, and that refusal now matches LLR-153's text. Every cited case exists.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] TC-156 Expected and Method -> before: the handback suite's report-first, loud-no-report, refused-second-close and re-sweep arms, via LLR-161 -> after: the same, plus: a disposition owing a successor drafts one or adopts an already-filed superseding row, with no new id and with full-successor replay under LLR-153's replay clause and LLR-335's single-lineage operation -> not the same: a new discharge arm enters the verified set. Ready to bless. Both of sitting 006's points are answered. The citation now names LLR-153 and LLR-335, and the Method is trimmed to the handback arms its own adoption cases hold (close and record, an unaccepted adoption refused, a drafted successor beside an adoption still minting). That leaves the mint arms to TC-147, where LLR-153 is verified.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] LLR-312 Detail -> before: the consolidation close's preflight and ordered rewrites -> after: the same, plus an adopting consolidation verdict refuses before its first write -> not the same: a new refusal. Unchanged since sitting 006 blessed it: the Trust section's ruling stated as an observable refusal.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] TC-254 Method -> before: the consolidation mint and close arms -> after: the same, plus an adopting consolidation verdict refuses before its first write and leaves the lane tree byte-identical -> not the same: a new arm. Unchanged since sitting 006 blessed it: held by `test_a_consolidation_verdict_that_adopts_refuses_the_close`.\n\nVERDICT: MEANING rows=7\n\n## first-approval\n\nLLR-335, LLR-345, TC-360 and TC-383 are byte-identical to the text sitting 006 approved. The rows around them in the chain have moved only as the amendment section above records. None of those moves touches what these four state or verify: LLR-153's added refusal sits beside LLR-335's single lineage operation and does not overlap it. I approve all four again, on the reasons sitting 006 recorded.\n\n- [APPROVE] LLR-335 -> the obligation: an adoption is a column-zero fenced TOML block naming one distinct successor id. It is validated once at the close, in the tree that becomes the commit's parent, against exactly one open (draft, queued or active), strictly readable spec whose `supersedes` names the disposed row, and every other state and unreadable input refuses. The close writes the validated ids, in request order, as the closed spec's record in the same rewrite. Replay trusts only that record, joins recorded and drafted successors in one lineage operation, and refuses every unequal or missing request-and-record pair, a malformed record and an unreadable merged spec -> what its chain shows: it decomposes SR-148's new adoption arm and every Trust ruling on the request, record, binding and absent inputs; TC-360 drives each -> why it is ready: a refactor-proof, fail-closed contract with no code symbol and no `shall`, unchanged since sitting 006.\n- [APPROVE] LLR-345 -> the obligation: an adoption record may be authored only by the machinery close's own commit, or carried unchanged with its request in a parent. A merge is judged on its novel changes. The close's own commit is defined exactly, its only admitted paths outside `docs/work` being the inbound relinks its moves wrote under the shared byte-exact relink reading (sitting 016's FIX). Every other record change, a rewritten or removed request, and a requesting move without a record refuse, as does an unreadable parent -> what its chain shows: the Trust section's commit check rebuilt on the owner's route, with every arm held by TC-383, including the real-close relink beside a hand-written record and the unchanged peel -> why it is ready: one decision over a closed set of admitted shapes, naming only boundary paths, unchanged since sitting 006.\n- [APPROVE] TC-360 -> the obligation: drive the parser, record reader, close and merge sweep over fixtures, covering accepted adoptions recorded at close, every malformed fence, request, record, title and successor refusal, and replays that re-point dependents with drafts and read no history -> what its chain shows: 25 named, existing cases verifying SR-148, LLR-335 and IF-294, including the repository-wide census of `## Dispositions` sections -> why it is ready: an observable Expected with a case per LLR-335 clause, unchanged since sitting 006.\n- [APPROVE] TC-383 -> the obligation: drive the record rule over real commits, lane walks, staged trees and squash landings; the close and an unchanged carriage pass, and every other record change, rewritten request, invalid parent-tree adoption, requesting move without a record and unreadable parent refuse; merges are judged on novel changes only, and the real close admits only its exact inbound relinks -> what its chain shows: 21 named, existing cases matching the Done-when's commit-check arms one for one -> why it is ready: Expected names the admitted set and its closure, unchanged since sitting 006.\n\nOUTCOME: APPROVE rows=4\n\nSITTING: JUDGED kinds=amendment;first-approval\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1657,"cache_read_input_tokens":719558,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1657},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f031c6a5-ecf0-490f-9890-c7a8a87f92fc","uuid":"594dc563-05da-49bb-8415-72cb4e6126ac","timestamp":"2026-10-22T09:33:46.299Z","request_id":"req_011CgH3b94mQ6ytVxzrfYcUp","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_014YAQt5ngVYBHPcSUSQajET":{"file_path":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-969\\docs\\reviews\\wi-969\\007-ADJUDICATE-9d73112.md","content":"# WI-969 checkpoint sitting at 9d73112\n\n## amendment\n\nThis sitting re-sits the set of sitting 006-ADJUDICATE-e213f8d. That sitting blessed SR-148, LLR-161, LLR-312 and TC-254, and returned LLR-153, TC-147 and TC-156. Commit 9d73112ca answers the returns. I compared every judged row at e213f8d7f and at this tip, field by field. Only the three returned rows changed, and only in the cells returned: LLR-153's Detail, TC-147's Expected, and TC-156's Expected and Method. The other four amended rows are byte-identical to the text blessed at 006. All seven rows changed MEANING against their anchor (b70ecbe9e), and I would bless all seven. The